  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through Sunday)\nIssued at 313 AM EST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\nIMPACTS CONTINUE...\n\nWith a stationary boundary directly overhead of the region, \nfrictional convergence (surface trough) directly along the east \ncoast, and a saturated vertical profile, the heavy rainfall threat \n(and setup) discussed above will persist across the east coast of \nSouth Florida on Wednesday and Thursday. But there may be light at \nthe end of the proverbial tunnel as the atmospheric log-jam finally \nbegins to give way during the second half of the week.\n\nThe placement of the right entrance region of a poleward sub-\ntropical jet streak over the western Atlantic waters and \nnorthwestern Bahamas will set the stage for an area of favorable \nupper level divergence. Model guidance (ECWMF & GEFS) has come into \nbetter agreement for the gradual development of a surface low along \na stationary frontal boundary in close proximity to South Florida \nduring this time frame as low level convergence is maximized. Model \nsolutions now depict a gradual deepening of the surface low pressure \nsystem with the low gradually lifting northwards beginning on \nThursday and continuing into the upcoming weekend. The development \nof a surface low to our southeast could eventually usher in a \nreinforcing surge of continental dry air via cyclonic flow around \nthe feature as it lifts further away from the region late in the \nwork week into the upcoming weekend. This may act to lift the \ngreatest threat of heavy rainfall to the north of the region by \nFriday and return the region to low end rain chances during the \nupcoming weekend. A gradual warming trend across the region with \nmaximum temperatures is forecast to continue during the second half \nof the week as temperatures are forecast to reach the low to mid 80s \neach afternoon through the upcoming weekend.",
